WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.590
SIDDHARTHA AGARWAL: Hi,
I'm Siddhartha Agarwal,

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.960
and I'm responsible for
our Saas partnerships

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.940
here at Google and
the co-innovation

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.280
that we do with our ISVs.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.770
I wanted to welcome you to
the session today, which

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.460
is going to talk about
how ISVs are accelerating

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.400
the digital transformation that
their customers are asking them

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.290
for and how these ISVs and Saas
companies are co-innovating

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.680
with Google to deliver
that transformation.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.990
And we also have a very
exciting customer story

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.350
to share with you on
how the ISV has actually

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.860
driven that co-innovation,
leveraging both Google

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.100
capabilities and GCP
capabilities or Google Cloud

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.170
Platform capabilities.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.830
So ISVs are being
asked to accelerate

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.250
the digital transformation
by their customers,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.950
and in order to
deliver this, the ISVs

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:58.650
are on a number
of cloud journeys.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.500
And I'd love to share with you
these cloud journeys and then

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.050
talk about some
customer examples

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:09.570
and also how Google is using
co-innovation to help the ISVs

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.600
on these cloud journeys.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.060
So starting off with the
multi-cloud expansion

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.810
side of it, most ISV
and Saas partners

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.850
are being asked
to leverage cloud

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.040
to deliver higher
availability, which

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.610
might mean delivering
an active-active DR site

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.430
or bursting into the
cloud or to modernize

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.350
their on-premise
applications, which

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.510
might involve lifting
and shifting existing

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.110
on-prem assets, or
actually moving out

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:39.750
of complete data centers,
or to expand into regions

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.770
with significant compliance and
data residency requirements,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.490
and most importantly, to
keep increasing the security,

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.900
their infrastructure security,
their user security, their data

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:52.650
security.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.610
Security is paramount.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:58.260
And so to help our ISVs
along these journeys,

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.130
we have built a number of
capabilities in our platform,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.870
for example, Anthos.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.700
[INAUDIBLE],, which is
a financial services

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.960
ISV, leveraged Anthos, which
is a financial services

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.040
application delivered on premise
to be able to actually burst

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.020
when additional
capacity is needed

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.380
into the cloud with an
active-active architecture

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.080
or be able to create
a DR scenario where,

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.540
in case something
went wrong on premise,

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.030
the application would be
live at any given point

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.280
in time from the public cloud.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.920
Also, Google's focused
a lot on plan and scale

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.650
by delivering the world's
largest network, which

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.310
was built for applications
like search, and YouTube,

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:43.510
and Gmail to enable ISVs to
reach their customers where

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:47.200
they are, and from a security
perspective, providing

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.020
innovations like
confidential compute using

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.110
AMD's in-memory encryption
and our tightened security

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:57.640
chips on our hardware to
deliver significant security.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:01.510
And openness is a
key tenet for Google,

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:03.940
where we have a longstanding
history of championing

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.450
open source and extending
that to Google Cloud by making

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.610
open source a
first-class citizen.

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:12.630
This could be for third-party
open source, such as Kafka

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.980
[INAUDIBLE] but as
you very well know,

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.560
also Kubernetes an
Istio, which has

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.920
led the transformation of
modernizing application

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.180
development.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:26.150
Every organization is focused
on innovating to differentiate,

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:28.870
and ISVs have to
innovate to compete

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:32.050
more effectively to grow
top line by expanding

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:34.220
into new markets.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:36.080
In order to do
this, they're asking

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.480
how intelligence can be
leveraged to make business

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.720
processes more automated
and predictable

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.630
without needing a lot
of data scientist skills

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.640
and how their users
can be engaged

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:48.620
via voice and messaging.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.120
So for example, in
intelligence, how

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.310
can they deliver better
business outcomes?

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.710
How can they get
insight from documents?

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:59.080
How can they do multilingual
translations in real time?

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.270
And user experience
is now no longer

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.700
about just browser-based
experiences,

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.700
so using virtual agents,
intelligence chat bots,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:11.180
and even search as the new
paradigm for interaction.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:13.300
And it's become
really important to be

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.890
able to use industry-specific
solutions, deliver

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.720
industry-specific solutions
to your customers,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.620
which means delivering
intelligence for retail

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:27.220
or delivering solutions that are
specific to financial services

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:29.150
or the public sector.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:31.730
Given the scale at which
date is being generated,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:33.610
insight from this
data can no longer

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:37.560
be derived with on-prem
traditional infrastructure,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.430
be it around compute
or around storage,

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:44.110
so customers are asking ISVs
how they can get insights from

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:48.320
and act upon data at
exabytes and petabytes scale.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.710
And this involves being
able to leverage streaming

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:54.490
data and batch data to be able
to make intelligent decisions

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:57.700
on the streaming data that is
coming in as the data is coming

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.040
in to be able to
blend first-party data

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.550
and third-party data,
first-party data that might be

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.890
the ISV's application and
third-party data that might be

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:08.830
coming from outside,
and enable business

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.920
users at these customers
to be able to make

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:14.170
those decisions
such that they don't

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:17.200
have to be data scientists,
they don't have to know AIML.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.230
They can just be getting
value from that data.

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.720
And then we're going to talk
about this in the customer

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.460
story or the ISVs story.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.140
It's really about
personalization

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:27.560
and segmentation.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.400
How can you be able to
personalize the engagement

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.202
with the customers
using broader data that

00:05:33.202 --> 00:05:34.160
could come from Google?

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.535
It could come from
Google Ads, it

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:37.810
could come from Google
Search, et cetera.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.090
Or being able to segment
the audience and being

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:43.460
able to target the audience
in the way you want--

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:45.970
So to that end, what
we've done is, one,

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:50.020
we're providing a very rich set
of AIML capabilities and very

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:53.840
strong AIML capabilities
to be able to, for example,

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.980
get insight from vision,
from speech, et cetera.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:00.580
But we've also built
solutions on top of the AI.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.270
So for example, we've built
a contact center AI solution

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:07.930
where in real time, as
customers are talking,

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.750
we're understanding the
intent of those customers

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.390
and having a virtual
agent be able to engage

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:16.210
with that customer
across multiple turns

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:19.150
without needing an agent
involved at all, being

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.240
able to do that in real time,
where the data is running

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:25.660
on our TPUs, the analysis
is running on our TPUs,

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:28.500
being able to get insight
from the conversations that

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:29.500
are being had.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:32.770
And if the answer cannot be
solved by the virtual agent

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:36.520
themselves, being able to
transfer that over to a live

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.880
agent and being able to provide
that agent with intelligent

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.170
insight on how to respond and
what is the next best action

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:46.650
that they should be suggesting.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:49.170
Similarly from documents,
being able to get insight

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:54.540
from documents, for example,
from a mortgage document.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.810
How do you understand what is
the rate, what is the financing

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:59.040
terms, et cetera?

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:00.810
Or from license, being
able to understand,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.830
is this a valid
driver's license?

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:05.520
Which state does this
driver's license belong to?

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.170
Or from contracts,
being able to get

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:10.230
the sense of what terms
have been agreed to,

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:13.540
limitation of
liability, et cetera.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:15.670
And then from an
analytics lens, being

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.610
able to provide you
the ability to stream

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:21.340
in data at large
scale into BigQuery,

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:24.790
where we've separated out
compute and storage, such

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:27.850
that you can analyze
this data at scale,

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:31.960
blend the data at scale
with petabytes of data being

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:35.170
crunched on at the same time.

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:37.590
And then you can actually
build monetization models

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.600
on top of this data,
where customers

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:42.450
who have really large amounts
of data that is really

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:43.890
valuable data that
they want to be

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:47.580
able to share out and monetize,
they can actually do very

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.740
granular access to that data.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:52.920
They can do governance and
access control to the data,

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:56.430
and they can monetize the
data from BigQuery itself,

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.500
so lots of really
cool capabilities that

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.570
are being delivered
for both of those

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:03.970
journeys to differentiate
with innovation

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.790
and to be able to get
insights from real-time data.

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:10.440
And what's really exciting
is how ISVs have not only

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.700
been doing co-innovation
with Google Cloud

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:16.260
Platform, but the broader
Google properties.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:20.140
That includes Google Pay, Google
Shopping, Android, Chrome, et

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:21.720
cetera.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.240
For example, in retail,
ISVs are looking

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.400
at how they can
leverage Google shopping

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.720
to be able to optimize the
price that is being offered up

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.390
by merchants that are
selling on their platform

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:36.720
or to be able to improve the
merchant discoverability that

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.950
exists for merchants on their
platform onto Google Shopping.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:42.990
And we'll be talking
about how Google Ads are

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:46.160
being used by that ISV.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.830
And also, these ISVs are
working on delivering

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:52.880
very smart industry and
horizontal solutions

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.720
by embedding intelligence to
deliver business processes that

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:00.890
are much better in understanding
what should be happening,

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:05.580
what is the customer looking for
in various specific industries.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.600
Now, one of the key areas is
around developer engagement.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:11.030
Developers, as we know,
are the new kingmakers,

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.790
and everyone is focused on
how to get these developers

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:15.660
attached to the platform.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:19.100
And so ISVs are looking at
how we can help the developers

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.220
upscale with new technologies
such as containerization,

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:26.480
microservices, AIML, and
really on DevOps and SRE

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:28.910
because we're delivering
applications at scale,

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:32.960
like Gmail, and how can they
improve their DevOps and SRE

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.430
capabilities to deliver that
same capability at large scale.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:41.060
What's really interesting is we
found the developers are trying

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:44.000
to provide insight
into product analytics

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.400
to the product manager,
so which features

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.770
are being used, what
functionality is being used,

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:51.140
and what are some informed
intelligent recommendations,

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:54.690
and how to enhance the
usage of the product.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:58.590
And then no Saas application or
no application out of the box

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:01.000
delivers all the
value that is needed,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.420
so ISVs are also looking at
building a rich extension

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.210
platform, for example,
leveraging our Apigee

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.970
capabilities to
provide API exposure

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:12.180
and to be able to monetize
and manage those APIs

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:14.400
or to provide business
logic extensions that

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.672
can ride alongside the
ISV or SaaS application.

00:10:19.672 --> 00:10:22.130
And then we're very focused on
partnering for joint success

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:23.550
with the ISVs.

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:25.010
The ISVs have
asked us what we're

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:28.190
doing to invest in developing
and innovating with you,

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:31.880
how we can increase the joint
go-to-market with the ISVs,

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.480
and how we can accelerate
distribution with the ISVs.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.730
So we're making
co-investment decisions

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:40.010
to provide unique
product engineering

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:43.080
of co-innovation funds,
defining joint go-to-market

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.560
with joint verticalization,
market expansion

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:49.550
planning, or global, regional
field alignment and accelerated

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:52.050
distribution, being able to
leverage the Google field,

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.590
being able to leverage the
Google Cloud Marketplace.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:56.690
And finally, executive
alignment and a focus

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:58.280
on customer success--

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:00.260
how do we make sure
that our customers

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:04.350
are going live, are onboarding,
and being really successful?

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:06.650
So these are the ways that
we're working with the ISVs

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:09.230
to enable their
journey to the cloud

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:11.870
to deliver multi-cloud
capabilities, to deliver

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:14.300
intelligence embedded
within their own solutions,

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:17.450
including for
industry-specific use cases,

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:19.640
and driving co-innovation
that's not just

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:24.200
about Google Cloud but also the
broader Google product areas.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:28.400
With that, I'm really excited
to invite to this discussion

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:31.130
Guy Hellier from OpenText,
whose team has done

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:35.150
an amazing job at driving
co-innovation to deliver value

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:36.590
to their customers.

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:37.248
Welcome, Guy.

00:11:37.248 --> 00:11:38.540
GUY HELLIER: Hello, Siddhartha.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:40.530
Thank you very much.

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:43.070
SIDDHARTHA AGARWAL: Great to
have you on this discussion

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:43.707
here.

00:11:43.707 --> 00:11:45.290
So can you just do
a little background

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.550
on yourself and OpenText?

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:48.300
GUY HELLIER: Sure.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:51.260
I'm responsible for product
management in our Digital

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:52.940
Experience product unit.

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:56.300
OpenText is a very large
information management

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:59.480
solutions company, and so we
focus on digital experience,

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:04.010
content services, business
network, information security,

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:06.910
and how that all helps our
customers to transform.

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:08.630
And in the area of
digital experience,

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:12.350
that's really about
helping our brand customers

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:15.950
be able to drive
conversions for customers

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:19.310
through digital
experience to grow revenue

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:21.590
and to do that on all
the different channels

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:24.080
that a customer journey and
touch points that a customer

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:25.730
journey occurs on.

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:27.480
SIDDHARTHA AGARWAL:
That's wonderful, Guy.

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:29.022
So it sounds like
marketers are going

00:12:29.022 --> 00:12:30.750
to be very happy
with your solution

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:33.060
because it drives higher
conversion and top

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:34.510
line for them.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:38.330
So can you share an example of
how this might be experienced

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:41.380
both by an end customer
of your customer

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:43.980
and by the marketing
organization in your customer?

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:44.730
GUY HELLIER: Sure.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:47.740
So let's take
purchasing a house.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:54.970
And as a target customer of
mine is purchasing a house,

00:12:54.970 --> 00:13:00.330
then they're looking for a
new house insurance provider.

